  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts` and it passes `typecheck`. I haven't run `vite build` or loaded the scene in a browser, so none of the behaviour below has been seen running.\n\n- **Scene:** a static floor and a 15-box pyramid (rows of 5, 4, 3, 2, 1) of dynamic rigid-body cubes, each with its own runtime id. There is also a heavy dynamic sphere (mass 30) that rests on the floor about 11 units in front of the pyramid.\n- **Lighting and camera:** a neutral environment light, a hemisphere light, and a directional \"sun\" with a 2048 shadow map fitted around the pyramid. The boxes and ball cast shadows and the floor receives them. A perspective camera frames both the pyramid and the ball's path.\n- **Physics:** the engine's app physics world steps from the frame callback at a fixed 1/60 s, and `bindNodes` writes each body's pose back to its mesh. About 1.5 s after the app is ready the ball is launched toward the pyramid at 21 m/s. The boxes should then tumble and settle under the physics solver.\n- **Space:** pressing Space calls `app.setScene(buildScene())`, which rebuilds the pyramid and ball, then relaunches after 0.4 s. I rebuilt the scene because the body handles can't reset a body's rotation.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__ = true` is set after two animation frames following `app.ready()`.\n\nThree things to know when testing:\n- The launch speed, the ball's mass, and the 0.4 s relaunch delay are values I picked without watching the crash, so they may need tuning.\n- The 1.5 s launch timer starts when the app is ready, not at page load.\n- I couldn't tell whether the engine also steps physics on its own, which would make the simulation run faster than real time. If the crash looks too fast, that is the place to check.\n\n",
